    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's a relief to hear there's a physical explanation for it—it's easy to let the imagination run wild in a place like that. \n\nthough, i did have to laugh when my site manager mentioned the \"scuttling\" sensation. he's actually been a bit of a jokester about it, saying it's like the building has its own little \"gremlins\" or something that come out at night to mess with the cleaning crew. \n\njust so i can shut him down with some actual facts when he brings it up again—is there any actual biological precedent for this in large commercial buildings? like, is it even plausible that something small, like a rodent or even some kind of insect, could leave behind those kinds of \"rhythmic\" or \"scurrying\" marks on a surface as hard as stone or concrete? or is it truly just air currents and settling?",
      "source": "evaluator"
